### [DE-01] Linear Regression Zero-Division Creating `NaN` Coordinates in Chart SVG
- **Location:** `new/client/src/pages/DashboardEnhanced.jsx`, Lines 49–59
- **Category:** UI Rendering / Arithmetic Edge Case
- **Code Snippet:**
  ```javascript
  49: const slope = (n * sumXY - sumX * sumY) / (n * sumXX - sumX * sumX);
  50: const intercept = (sumY - slope * sumX) / n;
  ...
  58: const emissions = Number((slope * year + intercept).toFixed(2));
  59: forecast.push({ year, forecast: Math.max(0, emissions) });
  ```
- **Explanation:** If `n <= 1` or if all historical data records have the same year, the denominator `(n * sumXX - sumX * sumX)` equals `0`. This results in `slope = NaN` and `emissions = NaN`. When `trendArray` feeds these points into the SVG `<path>` generator, `d="M NaN NaN L NaN NaN"` is generated, causing React SVG rendering warnings and blank charts.
- **Corrected Code:**
  ```javascript
  const calculateForecast = (data) => {
    if (!data || data.length < 2) return [];
    const n = data.length;
    let sumX = 0, sumY = 0, sumXY = 0, sumXX = 0;
    data.forEach((p) => {
      sumX += p.year;
      sumY += p.emissions;
      sumXY += p.year * p.emissions;
      sumXX += p.year * p.year;
    });

    const denom = n * sumXX - sumX * sumX;
    if (denom === 0) return []; // Avoid division by zero

    const slope = (n * sumXY - sumX * sumY) / denom;
    const intercept = (sumY - slope * sumX) / n;
    if (isNaN(slope) || isNaN(intercept)) return [];

    const lastYear = data[data.length - 1].year;
    const forecast = [];
    for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
      const year = lastYear + i;
      const emissions = Number((slope * year + intercept).toFixed(2));
      forecast.push({ year, forecast: Math.max(0, isNaN(emissions) ? 0 : emissions) });
    }
    return forecast;
  };
  ```

